Here's an example of how you can create a function in HTML to display a blinking text on a webpage, with a delay, custom blink interval, and random color change:

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
    <title>Blinking Text Example</title>
    <style>
        .blink {
            animation: blinker 0.5s step-start infinite;
        }

        @keyframes blinker {
            50% {
                opacity: 0;
            }
        }
    </style>
    <script>
        function blinkText() {
            var text = document.getElementById("blinkingText");
            var colors = ["red", "blue", "green", "yellow", "orange"]; // Define your set of colors here
            var currentColor = 0;
            setTimeout(function () {
                setInterval(function () {
                    text.style.color = colors[currentColor];
                    currentColor = (currentColor + 1) % colors.length;
                    text.classList.toggle("blink");
                }, 2000); // Change blink interval to 2000ms (2 seconds)
            }, 5000); // Start blinking after a delay of 5000ms (5 seconds)
        }
    </script>
</head>
<body onload="blinkText()">
    <h1 id="blinkingText">This text is blinking</h1>
</body>
</html>
```

In this example, we define a CSS class called "blink" with an animation that toggles the opacity of the text, creating the blinking effect. The JavaScript function `blinkText()` is called when the page loads. Inside the function, we use `setTimeout()` to delay the start of the blinking for 5 seconds. Then, we use `setInterval()` to set the blinking interval to 2 seconds (2000ms), and inside that interval, we toggle the "blink" class, change the color of the text to a random color from the predefined set, and update the current color index.

