The provided code incorrectly calculates the Julian Day Number, leading to incorrect results for the day of the week. Your task is to correct the code and implement the necessary leap year rules of the Julian calendar to accurately determine the day of the week for any given date.

function isLeapYear(year) {
  if (year % 4 === 0) {
    if (year % 100 === 0) {
      if (year % 400 === 0) {
        return true;
      }
      return false;
    }
    return true;
  }
  return false;
}

function getDayOfWeek(date) {
  const daysOfWeek = ['Sunday', 'Monday', 'Tuesday', 'Wednesday', 'Thursday', 'Friday', 'Saturday'];

  const year = date.getFullYear();
  const month = date.getMonth() + 1;
  const day = date.getDate();

  const a = Math.floor((14 - month) / 12);
  const y = year - a;
  const m = month + 12 * a - 2;

  const julianDayNumber = (day + y + Math.floor(y / 4) - Math.floor(y / 100) + Math.floor(y / 400) + Math.floor((31 * m) / 12)) % 7;

  return daysOfWeek[julianDayNumber];
}

Here is an implementation of the class in Java:

```java
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.Collections;
import java.util.Comparator;
import java.util.List;

public class Student {
    private String name;
    private int age;
    private String course;
    private double gpa;

    public Student(String name, int age, String course, double gpa) {
        this.name = name;
        this.age = age;
        this.course = course;
        this.gpa = gpa;
    }

    public String getName() {
        return name;
    }

    public void setName(String name) {
        this.name = name;
    }

    public int getAge() {
        return age;
    }

    public void setAge(int age) {
        this.age = age;
    }

    public String getCourse() {
        return course;
    }

    public void setCourse(String course) {
        this.course = course;
    }

    public double getGpa() {
        return gpa;
    }

    public void setGpa(double gpa) {
        this.gpa = gpa;
    }

    public static double calculateCumulativeGPA(List<Student> students) {
        double totalGpa = 0;
        double totalCredits = 0;
        for (Student student : students) {
            totalGpa += student.getGpa() * student.getCreditsEarned();
            totalCredits += student.getCreditsEarned();
        }
        return Math.round(totalGpa / totalCredits * 100) / 100.0;
    }

    public char getLetterGrade() {
        if (gpa >= 3.67) {
            return 'A';
        } else if (gpa >= 3.00) {
            return 'B';
        } else if (gpa >= 2.00) {
            return 'C';
        } else if (gpa >= 1.00) {
            return 'D';
        } else {
            return 'F';
        }
    }

    public static void sortStudentsByGpa(List<Student> students) {
        Collections.sort(students, new Comparator<Student>() {
            public int compare(Student s1, Student s2) {
                return Double.compare(s2.getGpa(), s1.getGpa());
            }
        });
    }

    public static void stableSortStudentsByGpa(List<Student> students) {
        Collections.sort(students, new Comparator<Student>() {
            public int compare(Student s1, Student s2) {
                int cmp = Double.compare(s2.getGpa(), s1.getGpa());
                if (cmp == 0) {
                    cmp = s1.getName().compareTo(s2.getName());
                }
                return cmp;
            }
        });
    }

    public static void sortStudentsByCriteria(List<Student> students, Comparator<Student> comparator) {
        Collections.sort(students, comparator);
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Student> students = new ArrayList<>();
        students.add(new Student("John Doe", 20, "Computer Science", 3.5));
        students.add(new Student("Jane Smith", 21, "Mathematics", 3.8));
        students.add(new Student("Bob Johnson", 19, "Physics", 3.2));

        System.out.println("Original List:");
        for (Student student : students) {
            System.out.println(student.getName() + " - " + student.getGpa());
        }

        System.out.println("\nSorted List by GPA (Descending Order):");
        sortStudentsByGpa(students);
        for (Student student : students) {
            System.out.println(student.getName() + " - " + student.getGpa());
        }

        System.out.println("\nStable Sorted List by GPA (Descending Order):");
        stableSortStudentsByGpa(students);
        for (Student student : students) {
            System.out.println(student.getName() + " - " + student.getGpa());
        }

        System.out.println("\nSorted List by Multiple Criteria (GPA and Age in Descending Order):");
        sortStudentsByCriteria(students, new Comparator<Student>() {
            public int compare(Student s1, Student s2) {
                int cmp = Double.compare(s2.getGpa(), s1.getGpa());
                if (cmp == 0) {
                    cmp = Integer.compare(s2.getAge(), s1.getAge());
                }
                return cmp;
            }
        });
        for (Student student : students) {
            System.out.println(student.getName() + " - " + student.getGpa() + " - " + student.getAge());
        }
    }
}
```

This implementation defines a `Student` class with the required attributes: `name`, `age`, `course`, and `gpa`. It provides getter and setter methods for each attribute. 

The class also includes a `calculateCumulativeGPA` method that takes a list of students and calculates the cumulative GPA by considering the credits earned for each course. 

The `getLetterGrade` method returns the student's letter grade based on their GPA, following the standard grading scale.

The `sortStudentsByGpa` method sorts the list of students based on their GPA in descending order using the `Collections.sort` method and a custom `Comparator`. 

The `stableSortStudentsByGpa` method sorts the list of students based on their GPA in descending order while maintaining the order of students with the same GPA. This is achieved by adding an additional comparison based on the student's name.

The `sortStudentsByCriteria` method allows for sorting the list of students based on multiple criteria, such as GPA and age, using a custom `Comparator`.

The `main` method demonstrates the usage of the class by creating a list of students, sorting them based on GPA, sorting them based on GPA while maintaining stability, and sorting them based on multiple criteria. The sorted student records are then printed to the console.

Here is an implementation of the global function in Java that fulfills all the given requirements:

```java
public class RomanNumeralConverter {

    public static String convertToRoman(String input) {
        int num;
        try {
            num = parseNumber(input);
        } catch (NumberFormatException e) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Invalid input: " + input);
        }

        if (num <= 0 || num > 3999) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Input out of valid range: " + num);
        }

        StringBuilder roman = new StringBuilder();
        int[] values = {1000, 900, 500, 400, 100, 90, 50, 40, 10, 9, 5, 4, 1};
        String[] symbols = {"M", "CM", "D", "CD", "C", "XC", "L", "XL", "X", "IX", "V", "IV", "I"};

        for (int i = 0; i < values.length; i++) {
            while (num >= values[i]) {
                roman.append(symbols[i]);
                num -= values[i];
            }
        }

        return roman.toString();
    }

    private static int parseNumber(String input) {
        if (input.matches("-?\\d+")) {
            return Integer.parseInt(input);
        } else if (input.matches("-?\\d*\\.\\d+")) {
            return Math.round(Float.parseFloat(input));
        } else if (input.matches("-?0x[0-9a-fA-F]+")) {
            return Integer.parseInt(input.substring(2), 16);
        } else if (input.matches("-?0b[01]+")) {
            return Integer.parseInt(input.substring(2), 2);
        } else {
            throw new NumberFormatException();
        }
    }
}
```

To use the function, you can call `RomanNumeralConverter.convertToRoman(input)` where `input` is the number or string representation of a number that you want to convert to its Roman numeral equivalent.

Here are some examples of how you can use the function:

```java
