In the given examples, the input 2D arrays are rotated clockwise by 90 degrees to obtain the output arrays. The original matrix is modified in-place.

Here is the correct implementation of the solution:

```java
public class ArrayRotation {
    public static void rotateArray(int[][] matrix) {
        int n = matrix.length;
        for (int layer = 0; layer < n / 2; layer++) {
            int first = layer;
            int last = n - 1 - layer;
            for (int i = first; i < last; i++) {
                int offset = i - first;
                int top = matrix[first][i];
                matrix[first][i] = matrix[last - offset][first];
                matrix[last - offset][first] = matrix[last][last - offset];
                matrix[last][last - offset] = matrix[i][last];
                matrix[i][last] = top;
            }
        }
    }
}
```

The incorrect part of the implementation was missing the rotation of the element at `matrix[i][last]`. The corrected implementation performs the rotation in-place by swapping elements in the array.

In the outer loop, `layer` represents the layer of elements to be rotated. In each layer, the elements in the top row (`matrix[first][i]`), left column (`matrix[last - offset][first]`), bottom row (`matrix[last][last - offset]`), and right column (`matrix[i][last]`) are swapped to perform the rotation.

The algorithm rotates the elements layer by layer, starting from the outermost layer and moving towards the inner layers. This ensures that all elements are rotated correctly.

After rotating the array, the elements will be rearranged to achieve a clockwise rotation by 90 degrees.

